        return jumps\n","approach":"**Implicit BFS: the indices reachable in exactly `k` jumps form a contiguous window, so each BFS level is just an interval.** Maintain `cur_end`, the right edge of the window for the current number of jumps, and `farthest`, the maximum `i + nums[i]` seen inside that window. When the scan reaches `cur_end`, every index in the level has been examined; the next level is `(cur_end, farthest]`, so increment `jumps` and set `cur_end = farthest`.\n\n**Why it works:** BFS over indices with edges `i -> i+1..i+nums[i]` gives the minimum jump count, and because the edge set from each index is a prefix of what lies ahead, levels are intervals. Tracking only the interval's right end is therefore lossless. The loop stops at `n - 2` because reaching or covering the last index in the current window means no extra jump is needed.\n\n**Common mistakes:** iterating to `n - 1` (counts one jump too many when the last index itself is `cur_end`), the O(n^2) DP `dp[i] = 1 + min(dp[j])`, and greedily jumping to the index with the largest `nums[i]` rather than the largest reach `i + nums[i]`.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Return the sequence of jumps? (Record, for each level, which index produced `farthest`.) What if the last index might be unreachable? (Return -1 when `i > cur_end`, as in Jump Game.) Jumps have a cost and you want the cheapest path? (Then it is a shortest-path problem: DP or Dijkstra, the interval trick no longer applies.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["jump-game","minimum-number-of-refueling-stops","video-stitching"],"number":149,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def jump(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}